# Arrow majors this workspace deliberately tolerates. One per line:
#
#   <major> <ISO date> <why it is acceptable, and what would end it>
#
# An entry is a DECISION somebody wrote down, not a suppression. The date is what makes a stale one
# visible: an exception nobody has revisited in a year is a question, and a gate cannot ask it.
#
# EVERY ROW MUST NAME A MAJOR Cargo.lock HOLDS, and `cargo xtask check-arrow` fails on one that does
# not - including the engine's own row, which is here so the report can name a reason for every major
# present rather than because it is an exception. Delete an entry in the same change that closes the
# split it permitted: what rots is not the row, it is the paragraph beside it, and that paragraph is
# the only thing a reviewer reads to decide whether the exception is still earned. The gate still
# cannot read the DATE, so a current-looking entry from a year ago is on you.
#
# The rule these are exceptions to: the ENGINE's Arrow major is the workspace's Arrow major. A data
# source is replaceable and the engine is not, so every other Arrow-consuming dependency conforms to
# it, and downgrading the engine to match an adapter is not on the table.
#
# Before adding an entry, establish which problem you have, because they differ in urgency and the
# lock file cannot tell you: a DUPLICATE costs build time and supply-chain surface, while a TYPE
# BOUNDARY - first-party code holding a value from one major and handing it to something expecting
# the other - does not compile, or is undefined behaviour if forced across the C data interface. The
# test is whether any first-party crate NAMES the type.

